What Is Driving Demand for Wafer Microscopy?
More intricate device patterns make defect review and dimensional metrology harder to standardize. A mark found during optical review may require nanoscale investigation before an engineer can decide whether it originated in deposition, etch, lithography or handling. Laboratories therefore combine techniques rather than replace all microscopes with a single expensive platform. The useful output is a defensible diagnosis connected to a process decision.

Manufacturing organizations also need consistent measurements across lots, operators and sites. Automation can reduce repeated stage positioning and image interpretation effort, while improved sample preparation helps preserve fine structures for electron analysis. However, instruments impose different facility demands, calibration work, operator skills and limits on how many wafers can be examined within a production window.

